From d6e38687b1bbab0a6d6165d5f6171efad76d7455 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mauricio Chirino <1657723+mchirino89@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:29:32 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] feat(ios): turn foldable simulators with setOrientation

On an iPhone Duo simulator (Xcode 27.1), `setOrientation` reported
COMPLETED and turned neither display, open or closed. The XCTest driver
sets `XCUIDevice.shared.orientation`, but the Duo's displays follow an
orientation relay inside the simulator (locationd) that only Device
Hub's rotate buttons reach.

Those buttons post the same vendor-defined HID event as the hinge
slider, with source `orientation-picker-control`, type `enum` and a
string value. `orient_helper.c` posts it the way `hinge_helper.c` posts
the hinge angle, and on a simulator `setOrientation` now spawns it before
calling XCTest. Order matters: the relay turns the display
asynchronously, and with the helper second a screenshot taken straight
after the command came back black.

The event goes to every simulator. An iPhone 17 Pro (iOS 26.5) ignores
it and keeps turning through XCTest exactly as before. `UPSIDE_DOWN` has
no Device Hub value and is left to XCTest alone: the Duo stays as it
was, which is also what an iPhone's Safari does when told upside down.
Physical devices are unchanged.

Verified with Safari, `setOrientation` then `takeScreenshot`:
- Duo closed: outer display 1398x2034 -> 2034x1398 (LANDSCAPE_LEFT and
  LANDSCAPE_RIGHT), back with PORTRAIT; five turns in one flow, each
  screenshot taken right after the command already turned.
- Duo open flat: inner display 2853x2007 -> 2007x2853 (LANDSCAPE_LEFT,
  LANDSCAPE_RIGHT), back with PORTRAIT.
- iPhone 17 Pro: 1206x2622 -> 2622x1206, as before.

